  partition: { means: 'The partition function Z sums the Boltzmann weights of all states — the bridge between microscopic physics and thermodynamics.', explore: 'Z, free energy, and how all thermodynamic quantities derive from it.', what: 'Z = Σ e^(−E_i/kT). Once you know Z, you can compute energy, entropy, heat capacity — everything.', name: 'Partition — how the probability is divided among states.', physical: 'F = −kT ln Z (free energy). ⟨E⟩ = −∂ ln Z/∂β.', quick: 'Z is the master key: add up all states weighted by their Boltzmann probability, and every thermodynamic quantity falls out by differentiation.', physics: 'Probabilities p_i = e^(−E_i/kT)/Z; all averages follow.', math: 'S = k ln Z + ⟨E⟩/T; C = ∂⟨E⟩/∂T.', equations: [{ eq: 'Z = Σ_i e^(−E_i / kT)', label: 'Partition function' }, { eq: 'F = −kT ln Z', label: 'Free energy' }, { eq: '⟨E⟩ = −∂ ln Z / ∂β', label: 'Average energy', where: 'β = 1/kT' }], calculated: ['Free energy', 'Average energy', 'Entropy', 'Heat capacity'], used: 'All of statistical mechanics, chemistry, condensed matter.', observe: 'Z grows with temperature (more states accessible); peaks in C reveal phase transitions.', why: 'Higher T makes more states probable, increasing Z and changing all derived quantities.', assumptions: ['Equilibrium', 'Known energy spectrum'], limitations: ['Computing Z is hard for interacting systems (use Monte Carlo)'], controls: { 'Temperature (T)': { desc: 'Sets the Boltzmann weights.', unit: 'K' } }, viz: { 'Energy landscape': 'States weighted by e^(−E/kT).' } },
